Bell numbers are a sequence of numbers that appear in combinatorial mathematics, and they count the number of partitions of a set. A partition of a set is a way of dividing the set into non-empty, disjoint subsets.

### Quick Diagram

Consider a set with 3 elements: {a, b, c}. The partitions of this set are:

1. {a}, {b}, {c} (3 subsets)
2. {a, b}, {c} (2 subsets)
3. {a, c}, {b} (2 subsets)
4. {b, c}, {a} (2 subsets)
5. {a, b, c} (1 subset)

There are 5 partitions of the set {a, b, c}. This is the 3rd Bell number, B(3) = 5.

